# 0.c is input mapping only

**Rule (user, 2026-08-31): do not edit `0.c` unless the change is an input
mapping (按键操作). Everything else goes in `2.c`.**

## What 0.c is allowed to own

Exactly one thing: **which input selects which action**, expressed as a
`func_95` call inside the `func_143` button chain.

```c
func_95(<action hash>, <arg1>, <flags>, <class index>);
```

That includes:

- which `global48` button bit / `global2` stick bit takes which branch
- which action hash a branch submits
- the four `func_95` arguments (they are one unit — see below)
- ammo / state gates that decide **whether** to submit at all
  (e.g. `sys_0(0x90000, slot)`, `sys_0(0xd0001, 0)`)

## What 0.c must never own

Behaviour. Form. Loadout. Motion. Phases. Cancel windows implemented as code.
All of that is `2.c`: the `func_241` registry, the `func_593` quartet,
`func_41` teardown, `func_882` keep-form. If you are tempted to "fix the move"
in `0.c`, you are in the wrong file.

## The four func_95 arguments are a weapon-category descriptor

They are not free choices. Hambrabi `0.c func_145` derives all four from the
action row, so a classic-selector unit must **copy them from an existing action
of the same category**, never hand-pick:

| arg | lands in | meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `arg0` | `global51` | action hash |
| `arg1` | `global61` | which pending queue: `func_88()` wants `0`, `func_87()` wants `1`. `0.c func_15` (the flight/idle form selector, `global20 & 0x4000` → index `0x18`) reads `func_88()` first, so keep-form flight actions need `arg1 = 0` |
| `arg2` | `global43` | cancel-route flags; `0x800` also flips `global22` |
| `arg3` | `global62` | class index = input slot. Published as shared field `0x1a` and used as a bit index (`0x1 << global62`) |

Wing Zero Rebellion class map, from its own 36 `func_95` calls:

```text
0   main-shot family AND every bird-form ranged action
    (0x476fac14 bird main, 0x2194f05d bird CS2, 0xd94d608f flight special)
1   N melee      2/3/4/5  front/left/right/back melee     6  BD melee
7   GROUND sub   8  ground special   9  special melee
0xa awakening    0xb CSA             0xc CSB
```

Copying `(0x1, 0x1, 0x7)` — the ground sub slot — onto a bird-form shot is what
routed it into native `func_15` → `func_882` and rebuilt the ground loadout.

## Diagnostic order when a move misbehaves

Read `0.c` **first**, edit `2.c` first.

1. Which branch actually ran? Check every `else` in the chain, including the
   neutral-stick fallback. A `2.c` fix applied to an action the input never
   reaches reads as "the patch changed nothing".
2. Only then look at the `2.c` action body.

## Verifying a 0.c change reached the game

`0.c` compiles to `0.bscex`, which is a **separate repack** from `2.c` →
`2.dscex`. Changing only one and repacking only the other is silent.

Do not grep the packed `.bscex` / `.dscex` for a hash as a little-endian u32 —
constants are not stored in the clear and even untouched hashes return zero
hits. Decompile the packed artefact with `tools/mscdec.py` and read it.

## Why this rule exists

Three failed in-game cycles on the Rebellion flight sub-shot, all the same
shape: the `2.c` action was correct, the `0.c` mapping was not, and each
"no change" result was read as a `2.c` bug and answered with another `2.c` edit.
